I see that the `MigrateSession` class is internal and sealed, which means it's not accessible from outside the assembly where it's defined. 

To fix this issue, we need to make the `MigrateSession` class public or create a public interface that it implements. However, since we can't modify the original code, we can use the `InternalsVisibleTo` attribute to make the internal types visible to our test assembly.

Here's an updated version of the test code:

```csharp
using Xunit;
using Moq;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

namespace Garnet.cluster
{
    public class MigrateSessionTests
    {
        [Fact]
        public async Task BeginAsyncMigrationTaskAsync_LogsError_WhenTrySetSlotRangesAsyncFails()
        {
            // Arrange
            var loggerMock = new Mock<ILogger>();
            var migrateSession = new MigrateSession(loggerMock.Object);

            // Act
            await migrateSession.BeginAsyncMigrationTaskAsync();

            // Assert
            loggerMock.Verify(l => l.LogError(It.IsAny<string>(), It.IsAny<object[]>()), Times.Once);
        }

        [Fact]
        public async Task BeginAsyncMigrationTaskAsync_LogsError_WhenTryPrepareLocalForMigrationFails()
        {
            // Arrange
            var loggerMock = new Mock<ILogger>();
            var migrateSession = new MigrateSession(loggerMock.Object);

            // Act
            await migrateSession.BeginAsyncMigrationTaskAsync();

            // Assert
            loggerMock.Verify(l => l.LogError(It.IsAny<string>(), It.IsAny<object[]>()), Times.Once);
        }

        [Fact]
        public async Task BeginAsyncMigrationTaskAsync_LogsError_WhenReserveDestinationVectorSetsAsyncFails()
        {
            // Arrange
            var loggerMock = new Mock<ILogger>();
            var migrateSession = new MigrateSession(loggerMock.Object);

            // Act
            await migrateSession.BeginAsyncMigrationTaskAsync();

            // Assert
            loggerMock.Verify(l => l.LogError(It.IsAny<string>(), It.IsAny<object[]>()), Times.Once);
        }

        [Fact]
        public async Task BeginAsyncMigrationTaskAsync_LogsError_WhenMigrateSlotsDriverInlineAsyncFails()
        {
            // Arrange
            var loggerMock = new Mock<ILogger>();
            var migrateSession = new MigrateSession(loggerMock.Object);

            // Act
            await migrateSession.BeginAsyncMigrationTaskAsync();

            // Assert
            loggerMock.Verify(l => l.LogError(It.IsAny<string>(), It.IsAny<object[]>()), Times.Once);
        }
    }
}
```

And in the `AssemblyInfo.cs` file of the test project, add the following line:

```csharp
[assembly: InternalsVisibleTo("Garnet.cluster.Tests")]
```

This will make the internal types of the `Garnet.cluster` assembly visible to the `Garnet.cluster.Tests` assembly, allowing us to test the `MigrateSession` class.

If you can't modify the `AssemblyInfo.cs` file, you can also use the `InternalsVisibleTo` attribute in the `AssemblyAttributes.cs` file of the test project.

Please note that this is just a workaround and it's not recommended to use internal types in your tests. It's better to create public interfaces or classes that can be tested easily.
